{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"tanstack-highlight","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Highlight"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Highlight is a tiny, synchronous syntax highlighter designed specifically for blogs and documentation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It occupies the space between minimal JavaScript-only scanners and full grammar engines:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import only the languages your site uses."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run the same highlighter during SSR and after hydration."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emit one escaped, class-based HTML tree for every theme."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Highlight common embedded regions without bundling a grammar runtime."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decorate lines and character ranges with ordinary classes and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data-*"},{"type":"text","value":" attributes."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-another-highlighter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why another highlighter?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most syntax highlighters optimize for one of two different products:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Editors and exact grammar compatibility."}]},{"type":"text","value":" These tools support TextMate grammars, hundreds of languages, editor scopes, and VS Code themes. That capability has a real initialization and bundle cost."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A single tiny language scanner."}]},{"type":"text","value":" These tools can be extremely small, but may not cover a documentation site's mix of markup, shell, data, and framework files."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Highlight optimizes for a third product: valid code samples rendered repeatedly in documentation. It gives up automatic language detection, editor state, semantic language-service tokens, and TextMate compatibility in exchange for a small synchronous path that can run on both server and client."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"core-principles","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core principles"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"selective-by-default","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selective by default"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/highlight/core"},{"type":"text","value":" entry contains no shipped languages. Every language is an isolated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"LanguageDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":" under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/highlight/languages/*"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"isomorphic","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isomorphic"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no WebAssembly, worker, filesystem dependency, or asynchronous initialization. A highlighter created from the same registrations and options returns the same result in Node.js and the browser."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"one-semantic-html-tree","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One semantic HTML tree"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Highlighting emits stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"th-*"},{"type":"text","value":" classes instead of token colors. Light and dark themes are CSS variable sets applied to the same markup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"context-where-it-matters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Context where it matters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most languages use small priority patterns. JavaScript templates, JSX, markup embeddings, shell heredocs, and similar cases use focused stateful scanners. The library does not contain a general grammar interpreter."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"output","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Output"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { highlight } from '@tanstack/highlight'\n\nconst result = highlight(`const answer = 42`, { lang: 'ts' })\n\nresult.code\nresult.lang\nresult.tokens\nresult.html","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The HTML contract is one escaped "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<pre><code>"},{"type":"text","value":" tree:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<pre class=\"th-code th-code--ts\" data-language=\"ts\">\n  <code>...</code>\n</pre>","lang":"html"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-your-starting-point","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose your starting point"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continue to "},{"type":"link","href":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for package and runtime requirements."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"link","href":"quick-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to build a selective highlighter and theme."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read "},{"type":"link","href":"comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comparison"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to decide whether this tradeoff fits your project."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review "},{"type":"link","href":"language-support","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Language Support"}]},{"type":"text","value":" before adopting it for an existing docs corpus."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"tanstack-highlight","text":"TanStack Highlight","level":1},{"id":"why-another-highlighter","text":"Why another highlighter?","level":2},{"id":"core-principles","text":"Core principles","level":2},{"id":"selective-by-default","text":"Selective by default","level":3},{"id":"isomorphic","text":"Isomorphic","level":3},{"id":"one-semantic-html-tree","text":"One semantic HTML tree","level":3},{"id":"context-where-it-matters","text":"Context where it matters","level":3},{"id":"output","text":"Output","level":2},{"id":"choose-your-starting-point","text":"Choose your starting point","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n# TanStack Highlight\n\nTanStack Highlight is a tiny, synchronous syntax highlighter designed specifically for blogs and documentation.\n\nIt occupies the space between minimal JavaScript-only scanners and full grammar engines:\n\n- Import only the languages your site uses.\n- Run the same highlighter during SSR and after hydration.\n- Emit one escaped, class-based HTML tree for every theme.\n- Highlight common embedded regions without bundling a grammar runtime.\n- Decorate lines and character ranges with ordinary classes and `data-*` attributes.\n\n## Why another highlighter?\n\nMost syntax highlighters optimize for one of two different products:\n\n1. **Editors and exact grammar compatibility.** These tools support TextMate grammars, hundreds of languages, editor scopes, and VS Code themes. That capability has a real initialization and bundle cost.\n2. **A single tiny language scanner.** These tools can be extremely small, but may not cover a documentation site's mix of markup, shell, data, and framework files.\n\nTanStack Highlight optimizes for a third product: valid code samples rendered repeatedly in documentation. It gives up automatic language detection, editor state, semantic language-service tokens, and TextMate compatibility in exchange for a small synchronous path that can run on both server and client.\n\n## Core principles\n\n### Selective by default\n\nThe `@tanstack/highlight/core` entry contains no shipped languages. Every language is an isolated `LanguageDefinition` under `@tanstack/highlight/languages/*`.\n\n### Isomorphic\n\nThere is no WebAssembly, worker, filesystem dependency, or asynchronous initialization. A highlighter created from the same registrations and options returns the same result in Node.js and the browser.\n\n### One semantic HTML tree\n\nHighlighting emits stable `th-*` classes instead of token colors. Light and dark themes are CSS variable sets applied to the same markup.\n\n### Context where it matters\n\nMost languages use small priority patterns. JavaScript templates, JSX, markup embeddings, shell heredocs, and similar cases use focused stateful scanners. The library does not contain a general grammar interpreter.\n\n## Output\n\n```ts\nimport { highlight } from '@tanstack/highlight'\n\nconst result = highlight(`const answer = 42`, { lang: 'ts' })\n\nresult.code\nresult.lang\nresult.tokens\nresult.html\n```\n\nThe HTML contract is one escaped `<pre><code>` tree:\n\n```html\n<pre class=\"th-code th-code--ts\" data-language=\"ts\">\n  <code>...</code>\n</pre>\n```\n\n## Choose your starting point\n\n- Continue to [Installation](installation) for package and runtime requirements.\n- Use [Quick Start](quick-start) to build a selective highlighter and theme.\n- Read [Comparison](comparison) to decide whether this tradeoff fits your project.\n- Review [Language Support](language-support) before adopting it for an existing docs corpus.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Overview\n---\n\n# TanStack Highlight\n\nTanStack Highlight is a tiny, synchronous syntax highlighter designed specifically for blogs and documentation.\n\nIt occupies the space between minimal JavaScript-only scanners and full grammar engines:\n\n- Import only the languages your site uses.\n- Run the same highlighter during SSR and after hydration.\n- Emit one escaped, class-based HTML tree for every theme.\n- Highlight common embedded regions without bundling a grammar runtime.\n- Decorate lines and character ranges with ordinary classes and `data-*` attributes.\n\n## Why another highlighter?\n\nMost syntax highlighters optimize for one of two different products:\n\n1. **Editors and exact grammar compatibility.** These tools support TextMate grammars, hundreds of languages, editor scopes, and VS Code themes. That capability has a real initialization and bundle cost.\n2. **A single tiny language scanner.** These tools can be extremely small, but may not cover a documentation site's mix of markup, shell, data, and framework files.\n\nTanStack Highlight optimizes for a third product: valid code samples rendered repeatedly in documentation. It gives up automatic language detection, editor state, semantic language-service tokens, and TextMate compatibility in exchange for a small synchronous path that can run on both server and client.\n\n## Core principles\n\n### Selective by default\n\nThe `@tanstack/highlight/core` entry contains no shipped languages. Every language is an isolated `LanguageDefinition` under `@tanstack/highlight/languages/*`.\n\n### Isomorphic\n\nThere is no WebAssembly, worker, filesystem dependency, or asynchronous initialization. A highlighter created from the same registrations and options returns the same result in Node.js and the browser.\n\n### One semantic HTML tree\n\nHighlighting emits stable `th-*` classes instead of token colors. Light and dark themes are CSS variable sets applied to the same markup.\n\n### Context where it matters\n\nMost languages use small priority patterns. JavaScript templates, JSX, markup embeddings, shell heredocs, and similar cases use focused stateful scanners. The library does not contain a general grammar interpreter.\n\n## Output\n\n```ts\nimport { highlight } from '@tanstack/highlight'\n\nconst result = highlight(`const answer = 42`, { lang: 'ts' })\n\nresult.code\nresult.lang\nresult.tokens\nresult.html\n```\n\nThe HTML contract is one escaped `<pre><code>` tree:\n\n```html\n<pre class=\"th-code th-code--ts\" data-language=\"ts\">\n  <code>...</code>\n</pre>\n```\n\n## Choose your starting point\n\n- Continue to [Installation](installation) for package and runtime requirements.\n- Use [Quick Start](quick-start) to build a selective highlighter and theme.\n- Read [Comparison](comparison) to decide whether this tradeoff fits your project.\n- Review [Language Support](language-support) before adopting it for an existing docs corpus.\n"}
